Context (pp. 72-74): in the third version of 'Analysis situs', Grothendieck works with abstract combinatorial 'structures' M of figures or pieces. These carry relations: X ≪ Y (refinement or subordination), a compatibility/overlap relation (written X ≭ Y in the transcription), X || Y (strangers, i.e. disjoint), X ≼ Y (equivalence up to subdivision), and a decomposition comb(X) of X into elementary pieces in a set L, governed by axioms Ax L1–L3. On p.72 he says he does not want to presuppose that the various 'rapports' attached to non-compatible figures live inside one ambient P(P). Instead he introduces, as extra structure, a 'realization by points' of M in a set P.

Definition on this page: a realization is a map M → P(P), X ↦ |X| (the 'support' of X), such that:
(i) it is monotone for ≪ and ⊂;
(ii) for compatible X, Y, |X| ∩ |Y| = ⋃_{Z ∈ X∩Y} |Z|, so supports intersect exactly along the combinatorial intersection;
(iii) X || Y ⇔ |X| ∩ |Y| = ∅. The ⇒ direction follows from (ii); the margin notes that 'the force of the axiom is in ⇐';
(iv) X ≼ Y ⇒ |X| = |Y|;
(v) |X| = ⋃_{Z ∈ comb(X)} |Z|;
(vi) (a) the supports cover P, and (b) any two points are separated by some support (one in it, the other not, or the reverse). This is a T0 / Kolmogorov separation condition.

NB1: (vi)(b) is only a normalization. One can always pass to the quotient P/~, where x ~ y iff M_x = M_y and M_x = {X : x ∈ |X|}. This is the Kolmogorov (T0) quotient.

NB2: by (v), a realization is determined by its values |Z| on elementary pieces Z ∈ L, and one defines |X| for general X by (v). The axioms then translate:
- (iii) ⇔ (iii') [x || y ⇔ |x| ∩ |y| = ∅ for x, y ∈ L] plus Ax L2;
- (ii) ⇔ Ax L3 plus (iii');
- (i) becomes tautological;
- (iv) follows from the others (given Ax L2, L3).
So, assuming the L-axioms, the variant is a map L → P(P) subject only to (iii') and T0 separation. This is exactly the two-condition definition opening p.74.

What it serves (p.74): Grothendieck asks whether such support data determine P up to unique isomorphism. For instance, is there a canonical (anti-)isomorphism between an ordered set ΣM of 'abstract supports' of M and (a subset of) P(P)? The model example is segments (sub-strings) of a topological 'ficelle'. This is a representation-theorem problem: recover points from a region/part structure, in the spirit of Stone duality or point-free topology, inside his combinatorial approach to tame ('modérée') topology.
